<chapter xml:id="ch-dnd-anatomy" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Anatomy of a Drag-and-Drop Item</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A conventional autograded question records almost nothing about the act
      of answering it. A multiple-choice item stores the option chosen; a
      numeric-entry item stores the number typed. Each try is, in effect, a
      single correctness verdict, perhaps tagged with which wrong option was
      selected. The <em>process</em> by which the student arrived at that
      verdict is invisible.
    </p>

    <p>
      In contrast, a drag-and-drop item, fully logged, tracks every individual 
      placement of a card, every <xref ref="gt-reset" text="custom">reset</xref>, 
      and every answer <xref ref="gt-check" text="custom">check</xref> together
      with feedback and timestamps. We therefore have two layers of information
      rather than one.
    </p>

    <p>
      The <term>outcome</term> layer answers questions about the final answer:
      was it correct, on which check, with how much partial credit, and which
      decoys caught the student. The <term>process</term> layer answers
      questions about the path: did the student converge steadily toward the
      key or thrash among configurations, which cards were placed first and
      which were repeatedly second-guessed, where did long pauses occur, and
      did the student reason the answer out or instead probe the grader by
      checking after each small change.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-sample-question">
    <title>Sample Question</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Before analyzing drag-and-drop questions, it is helpful to see how one is
        constructed. The example introduced in this section is a small
        <xref ref="gt-cardsort" text="custom">cardsort</xref> that asks a student to
        match four shapes to their general category. It will serve as a running example 
        to illustrate the concepts throughout this document. 
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-cardsort-elements">
      <title>Cardsort Elements</title>
      <p>
        <xref ref="question-source"/> shows the <pretext/> source used to create our 
        sample question. Placing a <tag>cardsort</tag> element inside an <tag>exercise</tag>
        tells <pretext/> that this exercise should be a drag-and-drop question. The 
        <tag>premise</tag> elements define the text on the objects that the student will drag, 
        and the <tag>response</tag> elements define the text on objects that the student will 
        drop them onto. 
      </p>

      <p>
        Each <tag>premise</tag> <q>belongs</q> to the <tag>response</tag> in the same 
        <tag>match</tag> block. There can be any number of <tag>premise</tag> elements in a
        <tag>match</tag> block, but there can be at most one <tag>response</tag> element.
      </p>
      
      <listing xml:id="question-source">
        <title>The source code for the sample question.</title>
        <program line-numbers="yes">
          <![CDATA[ 
          <exercise>
            <statement>
              <p>
                Match each shape to its general category.
              </p>
            </statement>
            <cardsort>
              <match>
                <premise>equilateral triangle</premise>
                <premise>right triangle</premise>
                <response>Triangle</response>
              </match>
              <match>
                <premise>square</premise>
                <response>Quadrilateral</response>
              </match>
              <match>
                <response>Pentagon</response>
              </match>
              <match>
                <premise>circle</premise>
              </match>
            </cardsort>
          </exercise>
          ]]>
        </program>
      </listing>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-cardsort-key">
      <title>Cardsort Key</title>
      <p>
        The correct answer to a drag-and-drop question is called the 
        <xref ref="gt-key" text="title"/> and it is defined by matching each <tag>premise</tag> 
        to the <tag>response</tag> it belongs to (i.e., in the same <tag>match</tag> block).
        If a <tag>premise</tag> has no corresponding <tag>response</tag>, it is a card that 
        should remain unplaced. If a <tag>response</tag> has no corresponding <tag>premise</tag>, 
        it is a slot that should remain empty. The only rule is that every premise must either be 
        placed inside a response or left unplaced.
      </p>

      <p>
        From <xref ref="question-source"/>, we know:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <c>equilateral triangle</c> and <c>right triangle</c> belong to <c>Triangle</c>,
          </li>
          <li>
            <c>square</c> belongs to <c>Quadrilateral</c>,
          </li>
          <li>
            <c>Pentagon</c> is meant to be empty, and
          </li>
          <li>
            <c>circle</c> is meant to be unplaced.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        For ease of discussion, we will refer to the draggable objects (<tag>premise</tag>) as 
        <term>cards</term> and the drop areas (<tag>response</tag>) as <term>slots</term>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-rendered-board">
      <title>Rendered Board</title>
      <p>
        When the question's source in <xref ref="question-source"/> is built, it renders the 
        following Drag-and-Drop <term>board</term> that the student interacts with. The cards 
        are on the left, and the slots are on the right. The area that holds the cards before 
        they are placed is the <term>dragzone</term> or <term>tray</term>.
      </p>

      <p>
        A card sitting in the dragzone is
        <em><xref ref="gt-placed" text="custom">unplaced</xref></em>, and one sitting in a slot
        is <em><xref ref="gt-placed" text="custom">placed</xref></em>. A <term>zone</term> is
        any place a card can rest, so this board has four zones: the tray and the three slots.
        A complete snapshot of the board, recording which zone every card currently occupies, is
        a <term>configuration</term>, and the <term>key configuration</term> is the correct
        arrangement.
      </p>

      <!-- The solution figure lives inside the sample exercise, which is
           component="html" only; the PDF build drops it, so an unconditional
           xref would dangle there (audit D6). Each version references the
           figure that actually exists in its output. -->
      <p component="html">
        The key configuration is shown in the sample question's
        <xref ref="fig-motivation-solution" text="custom">expected
        solution</xref> below.
      </p>

      <p component="pdf">
        The rendered board and its key configuration are shown side by side
        in <xref ref="fig-motivation-exercise-pdf" text="custom">the
        rendered-board figure</xref>.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="sample-dnd" component="html">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Match each shape to its general category.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <cardsort>
          <match>
            <premise>equilateral triangle</premise>
            <premise>right triangle</premise>
            <response>Triangle</response>
          </match>
          <match>
            <premise>square</premise>
            <response>Quadrilateral</response>
          </match>
          <match>
            <response>Pentagon</response>
          </match>
          <match>
            <premise>circle</premise>
          </match>
        </cardsort>
        <solution>
          <title>Expected Solution</title>
          <figure xml:id="fig-motivation-solution">
            <caption>The solution to the four-card question.</caption>
            <image source="figures/rendered-cardsort-soln.png" width="90%"/>
          </figure>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <figure xml:id="fig-motivation-exercise-pdf" component="pdf">
        <caption>
          Rendered Initial and Solution Board for a Drag-and-Drop (<tag>cardsort</tag>) question.
        </caption>
        <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
          <image source="figures/rendered-cardsort.png" width="100%"/>
          <image source="figures/rendered-cardsort-soln.png" width="100%"/>
        </sidebyside>
      </figure>
      
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-basic-actions">
      <title>Basic Actions</title>
      <p>
        The student interacts with the board through three basic <term>actions</term>, and each one 
        is logged:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              A <term>move</term>: dragging a single card from one zone to
              another. A <xref ref="gt-move" text="custom">move</xref> whose destination is the 
              dragzone, putting a card back, is a <term>takeback</term>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              A <term>check</term>: pressing the <q>check</q> button. It moves
              nothing; it records the current <xref ref="gt-configuration" text="title"/> and 
              returns <xref ref="gt-feedback" text="custom">feedback</xref>, graded against the 
              <xref ref="gt-key" text="custom">key</xref>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              A <term>reset</term>: pressing the <q>reset</q> button, which in one step returns 
              every <xref ref="gt-card" text="custom">card</xref> to the 
              <xref ref="gt-dragzone" text="custom">dragzone</xref>, restoring the 
              <xref ref="gt-initial-state" text="custom">initial state</xref>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        A fourth less common action, the <xref ref="gt-page-view" text="custom">page view</xref>,
        is logged when the student first sees the question and when they return to it after 
        leaving the page.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-raw-data">
    <title>Raw Data</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Runestone logs every <xref ref="gt-action" text="custom">action</xref> a student takes on a 
        <xref ref="gt-cardsort" text="custom">cardsort</xref>, and the logs reach us as a tab-separated 
        (<c>.tab</c>) export through DataShop: the <term>raw data</term>. The export is a table with one 
        row per logged action, in time order, across all students, problems, and sessions in the course. 
        Sixteen columns appear in the current export; <xref ref="tab-datashop-columns"/> groups the ones 
        that matter for this study.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-datashop-columns">
        <title>The columns of the raw data export, grouped by role.</title>
        <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
          <col width="33%" halign="left"/>
          <col width="67%" halign="left"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>Column</p></cell>
            <cell><p>What it holds</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>Time</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Timestamp of the action, to the microsecond. Every timing
            quantity in this study, whether pauses,
            <xref ref="gt-move-gap" text="custom">gaps between moves</xref>, or
            <xref ref="gt-check-gap" text="custom">gaps between checks</xref>,
            is a difference of these.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>Anon Student Id</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The anonymized student, the unit every attempt belongs to.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>Session Id</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>One continuous sitting; separates work within one session from
            work resumed later.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>Problem Name</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The cardsort question the row belongs to, e.g. <c>bookID__questionID</c>.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>Problem View</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>A running counter of the student's logged interactions with
            that problem; it gives the within-problem order even if rows are
            shuffled.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>Selection</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>The kind of row: <c>dragNdrop-drop</c> for a move,
            <c>dragNdrop</c> for a check, <c>dragNdrop-reset</c> for a reset,
            and <c>page</c> for a page view. The export is not
            cardsort-only: other question types log their own values here
            (<c>shortanswer</c>, <c>mChoice</c>, <c>parsons</c>, …), which
            this study ignores.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>Action</c></p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                The output of the action. Its format depends on the trigger
                that generated it. Long values are <em>truncated</em> in the export,
                so a check row's configuration JSON can arrive cut off mid-object
                here and should be read from <c>Input</c> instead.
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>Input</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>On a check row, the complete configuration as JSON, never
            truncated <mdash/> the authoritative parse target; empty
            otherwise.</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>Feedback Text</c>, <c>Feedback Classification</c></p></cell>
            <cell>
              <p>
                The graded result of a check (<c>Correct</c> or <c>Incorrect</c>); empty on non-check 
                action rows.
              </p>
            </cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p><c>Class</c>, <c>Level (Chapter)</c>,
            <c>Level (SubChapter)</c>, <c>CF (...)</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Where the problem lives in the course, plus custom fields
            (code, week number, institution); bookkeeping rather than
            behavior.</p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-positional-labels">
      <title>Positional Labels</title>
      <p>
        The <xref ref="gt-raw-data" text="title"/> never uses the authored names of the 
        <xref ref="gt-card" text="custom">cards</xref> and 
        <xref ref="gt-zone" text="custom">zones</xref>. Instead, it assigns generic labels of the form:
        <ul>
          <li><em>card</em>: <c>bookID__questionID_drag#</c></li>
          <li><em>zone</em>: <c>bookID__questionID_drop#</c></li>
        </ul>
        and attaches a <term>positional</term> index on the <c>#</c> corresponding to order of the element in the 
        question's source. The only exception is the <xref ref="gt-dragzone" text="custom">dragzone</xref>, which 
        is not indexed and whose label is the literal <c>dragzone</c> (see <xref ref="tab-raw-data-labels"/>).
        Decoding <xref ref="gt-positional-labels" text="custom">positional labels</xref> back to authored names is
        therefore the first step of every analysis, and it requires the question source.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-raw-data-labels">
        <title>Card/Zone Data Labels (<c>*</c>=<c>bookID__questionID</c>)</title>
        <tabular halign="center">
          <col width="30%" />
          <col width="18%" right="medium"/>
          <col width="27%" />
          <col width="18%" />
          <row header="yes">
            <cell colspan="2">Cards</cell>
            <cell colspan="2">Zones</cell>
          </row>
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell><p>text label</p></cell>
            <cell><p>data label</p></cell>
            <cell><p>text label</p></cell>
            <cell><p>data label</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>equilateral triangle</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>*_drag1</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>Triangle</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>*_drop1</c></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>right triangle</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>*_drag2</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>Triangle</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>*_drop1</c></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>square</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>*_drag3</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>Quadrilateral</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>*_drop2</c></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><c>circle</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>*_drag4</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><mdash/></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>dragzone</c></p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p><mdash/></p></cell>
            <cell><p><mdash/></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>Pentagon</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>*_drop3</c></p></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-outcome-and-process-layers">
      <title>Outcome and Process Layers</title>
      <p>
        <xref ref="tab-raw-data-trace"/> assembles these row types into a short illustrative <xref ref="gt-attempt" text="custom">attempt</xref> 
        where the columns are abbreviated and the timestamps shown as elapsed time to fit the page. The student places three cards quickly, 
        hesitates, places the circle in Pentagon, checks and is told <c>Incorrect</c>, takes the circle back, and checks again.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="tab-raw-data-trace">
        <title>An illustrative attempt as it would appear in the raw data
        (bookkeeping columns omitted; <c>*_</c> labels abbreviated).</title>
        <tabular halign="center">
          <col width="10%"/>
          <col width="26%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="26%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="14%" halign="center"/>
          <col width="14%" halign="center"/>
          <row bottom="medium">
            <cell><p><c>Time</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>Selection</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>Action</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>Feedback</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p>Type</p></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>0:00</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>dragNdrop-drop</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>drag1 -&gt; drop1</c></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>move</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>0:06</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>dragNdrop-drop</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>drag2 -&gt; drop1</c></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>move</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>0:11</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>dragNdrop-drop</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>drag3 -&gt; drop2</c></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>move</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>0:31</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>dragNdrop-drop</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>drag4 -&gt; drop3</c></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>move</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>0:35</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>dragNdrop</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>{ JSON object }</c></p></cell>
            <cell><c>Incorrect</c></cell>
            <cell>check</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell><p>0:51</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>dragNdrop-drop</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>drag4 -&gt; dragzone</c></p></cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>move</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell><p>0:55</p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>dragNdrop</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>{ JSON object }</c></p></cell>
            <cell><p><c>Correct</c></p></cell>
            <cell>check</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        The check actions provide the outcome layer: two checks, the first 
        <c>Incorrect</c> and the second <c>Correct</c>, each carrying the 
        full configuration that was graded. Every other row is the process 
        layer: the order of placements (the two triangles and the square in 
        eleven seconds, then a twenty-second hesitation before the circle 
        was forced into Pentagon), and the takeback that responded directly 
        to the feedback.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <c>Selection</c> and <c>Action</c> columns identify the type of 
        layer. For example, the second recorded entry of 
        <xref ref="tab-raw-data-trace"/> shows a <term>move</term> with
        <ul>
          <li><c>Selection</c><m>\to</m><c>dragNdrop-drop</c>, and</li>
          <li><c>Action</c><m>\to</m><c>bookID__questionID_drag2 -&gt; bookID__questionID_drop1</c>.</li>
        </ul> 
        In terms of the running example, this translates to 
        <md>
          \overset{\large\text{card 2}}{\boxed{\text{right triangle}}} \to\ \overset{\large\text{zone 1}}{\boxed{\text{Triangle}}}
        </md>.
      </p>

      <p>
        The second to last entry illustrates a move from card 4 to <c>dragzone</c>, the tray.
      </p>

      <p>
        A <term>Reset</term> (not shown in <xref ref="tab-raw-data-trace"/>) is identified by
        <ul>
          <li><c>Selection</c><m>\to</m><c>dragNdrop-reset</c>, and</li>
          <li><c>Action</c><m>\to</m><c>reset</c>.</li>
        </ul> 
      </p>

      <p>
        There are two <term>checks</term> in <xref ref="tab-raw-data-trace"/>, denoted by:
        <ul>
          <li><c>Selection</c><m>\to</m><c>dragNdrop</c>, and</li>
          <li><c>Action</c> and <c>Input</c><m>\to</m><c>{ JSON object }</c>.</li>
        </ul>
        where the JSON object is a recording of the current board configuration.
        The same JSON is logged in both columns, but the export truncates long
        <c>Action</c> values and a wide JSON can be cut off mid-object. Therefore, 
        <c>Input</c> is the authoritative copy every 
        <xref ref="subsec-event-payloads" text="custom">event payload</xref>. For 
        example, the last logged row has an <c>Input</c> value containing:
      </p>

      <program language="json">
        <![CDATA[
        {
          "*_drop1": ["*_drag1","*_drag2"],
          "*_drop2": ["*_drag3"],
          "*_drop3": []
        }
        ]]>
      </program>

      <p>
        This JSON tells us that
        <ul>
          <li><c>drop1</c> (zone 1) holds <c>drag1</c> (card 1) and <c>drag2</c> (card 2)</li>
          <li><c>drop2</c> (zone 2) holds <c>drag3</c> (card 3)</li>
          <li><c>drop3</c> (zone 3) is empty, and</li>
          <li><c>dragzone</c> (tray zone 0) <em>implicitly</em> holds <c>drag4</c> (card 4)</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Note that some rows are logged with <c>Selection</c> equal to <c>page</c>. These entries
        mark the student opening the page containing the question. Although they are not part of
        any attempt, they help delimit <xref ref="gt-session" text="custom">sessions</xref> and
        <xref ref="gt-attempt" text="custom">attempts</xref>: under the default boundary rules,
        an intervening page view starts a new attempt
        (<xref ref="sec-session-and-attempt-boundaries" text="title"/>).
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <conclusion>
    <p>
      This chapter introduced a question as authored, the
      <xref ref="gt-board" text="custom">board</xref> as the student sees it, the 
      raw data as the platform records it. The next chapter turns these concrete 
      parts into a formal <term>item design</term>. It records the cards, slots, 
      and tray as index sets, encodes the answer key together with its decoy cards 
      and decoy slots, and treats the feedback policy as a design choice, holding 
      this fixed authored structure apart from any single student's trajectory.
    </p>
  </conclusion>
</chapter>